class TrieNode:
    def __init__(self):
        self.children = {}
        self.is_word = False

class Trie:
    def __init__(self):
        self.root = TrieNode()

    def insert(self, word):
        node = self.root
        for char in word:
            if char not in node.children:
                node.children[char] = TrieNode()
            node = node.children[char]
        node.is_word = True

    def find_longest_common_prefix(self):
        node = self.root
        prefix = ""

        while len(node.children) == 1 and not node.is_word:
            char = list(node.children.keys())[0]
            prefix += char
            node = node.children[char]

        return prefix

def longest_common_prefix(strings):
    trie = Trie()
    for string in strings:
        trie.insert(string)

    return trie.find_longest_common_prefix()

# Test the program
strings = ["HelloKong", "helloDog", "helloCat"]
print(longest_common_prefix(strings))
```

This program uses a trie data structure to efficiently find the longest common prefix among a list of strings. The program first inserts all the strings into the trie, and then uses the trie to find the longest common prefix by traversing down the trie until there is more than one child node or the current node represents a word. The time complexity of inserting all the strings into the trie is O(N*M), where N is the length of the list of strings and M is the average length of the strings. The time complexity of finding the longest common prefix is O(M), where M is the maximum length of the strings. The space complexity is also O(M), as the trie requires extra space to store the characters of the strings.

