To calculate the sine of a given angle without using built-in trigonometric functions, we can use the Taylor series expansion for sine:

sin(x) = x - x^3/3! + x^5/5! - x^7/7! + ...

The Taylor series expansion converges to the true value of sine as more terms are added. However, to optimize the algorithm to have a time complexity of O(log n), we can use the property of periodicity of the sine function:

sin(x + 2π) = sin(x)

We can use this property to reduce the given angle to a range of [-π/2, π/2] by repeatedly subtracting or adding 2π until the angle falls within this range. Then, we can use the Taylor series expansion to calculate the sine of the reduced angle.

Here is the implementation of the `customSin` function in JavaScript:

```javascript
function customSin(angle) {
  if (angle < 0) {
    throw new Error("The angle must be positive.");
  }

  // Reduce angle to the range [-π/2, π/2]
  while (angle > Math.PI / 2) {
    angle -= Math.PI;
  }
  while (angle < -Math.PI / 2) {
    angle += Math.PI;
  }

  // Calculate sine using the Taylor series expansion
  let result = 0;
  let term = angle;
  let sign = 1;
  let factorial = 1;

  for (let i = 1; i <= 10; i += 2) {
    result += sign * term / factorial;
    term *= angle * angle;
    factorial *= (i + 1) * (i + 2);
    sign *= -1;
  }

  return result.toFixed(5);
}
```

