To transform a deeply nested JSON object into a valid XML document with proper indentation and line breaks, we can use a recursive approach. Here's an example implementation in JavaScript:

```javascript
function jsonToXml(json, indent = '') {
  let xml = '';

  // Handle arrays
  if (Array.isArray(json)) {
    json.forEach((element) => {
      xml += jsonToXml(element, indent);
    });
    return xml;
  }

  // Handle objects
  if (typeof json === 'object') {
    for (const key in json) {
      if (json.hasOwnProperty(key)) {
        const value = json[key];

        // Handle attributes
        if (key.startsWith('@')) {
          const attributeName = key.slice(1);
          xml += ` ${attributeName}="${value}"`;
        } else {
          xml += `${indent}<${key}`;
          if (typeof value === 'object') {
            xml += `>\n${jsonToXml(value, `${indent}  `)}${indent}</${key}>\n`;
          } else {
            xml += `>${value}</${key}>\n`;
          }
        }
      }
    }
    return xml;
  }

  // Handle values
  return `${json}`;
}

// Example usage
const json = {
  root: {
    '@version': '1.0',
    '@encoding': 'UTF-8',
    element: 'value',
    nested: {
      array: [
        'item 1',
        'item 2'
      ]
    }
  }
};

const xml = jsonToXml(json);
console.log(xml);
```

This implementation handles arrays and objects recursively. For arrays, it iterates over each element and recursively calls `jsonToXml` on them. For objects, it iterates over each key-value pair. If the key starts with `@`, it is considered an attribute and added to the XML element. Otherwise, it is considered a child element and recursively processed.

